ls -R .claude"]], "final_message": "The cause is mixed timestamp formats in `events.csv`. The `ts` column has three different conventions:\n\n| id | ts | What it means |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `2026-03-01T10:00:00Z` | 10:00 UTC |\n| 2 | `2026-03-01T10:05:00` | No offset, so the zone is unknown |\n| 3 | `2026-03-01T04:10:00-06:00` | 10:10 UTC, written in local time at UTC-6 |\n\nIf all three were read correctly as UTC, they would fall within 10 minutes of each other (10:00, 10:05 and 10:10 UTC). The charts show jumps of several hours because the parser treats them differently:\n\n- **Row 2 has no offset.** Most tools assume the machine's local zone. pandas leaves it as a naive value, and JavaScript's `Date` treats it as local time. Depending on where the chart is built, it lands hours away from rows 1 and 3.\n- **Row 3 is correct but easy to misread.** If a tool drops the offset and keeps the wall-clock `04:10`, it plots 6 hours early. Mixing naive and aware values in one pandas column can also fall back to strings or raise an error. That can lead to code that strips the zone.\n\n**Fix**\n1. Ask whatever produces row 2 which zone it logs in. I can't tell that from the file. The `Z` and `-06:00` rows suggest at least two sources with different conventions.\n2. Normalize everything to UTC when you load it, with the zone for naive values stated explicitly. For example, in pandas: `pd.to_datetime(df.ts.where(has_offset, df.ts + \"Z\"), utc=True)`. Replace `\"Z\"` with the real offset if row 2 isn't UTC.\n3. Convert to a display zone only at chart time.\n4. Ideally, fix the exporter so every row is written as `...Z` or with an explicit offset.\n\nI only looked at the CSV. There's no chart code in this directory, so I haven't confirmed how your charting step parses it. If you point me to that script, I can check it and write the normalization. I can also run the `zz-timezone-audit-00443` skill for a fuller audit.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}